      "Networking and Operating system fundamentals\n420-ZX6-UM\nInstructor: Ehsan Shirazi\nMontreal College of Information Technology \n Coll\u00e8ge des technologies de l\u2019information de Montr\u00e9al\nEmail: ehsan.shirazi@faculty.montrealcollege.ca\nSession Learning Outcomes\nNetwork Communication Types Explained\nEthernet and Wi-Fi: Key Networking Technologies\nIntroduction to Frames, Packets and Segments/Datagrams\nExploring Key Network Devices\nGateway Explained\nIntroduction to IP Classes\nDefining Vlan and Subnetting\nVirtualization Explained\nTypes of Network Communication\nCommunication type in a network refers to the method by which data is transmitted between\ndevices, including unicast, broadcast, multicast, and anycast, each serving different purposes for\ndata delivery.\nUnicast\nDefinition: One-to-one communication.\nExample: Sending an email to a friend.\nBroadcast\nDefinition: One-to-all communication.\nExample: Sending an ARP request to all devices in a network.\nMulticast\nDefinition: One-to-many communication to a specific group.\nExample: Streaming a webinar to all registered participants.\nAnycast\nDefinition: One-to-nearest or best receiver among many.\nExample: Accessing a website directed to the nearest server.\nEthernet and Wi-Fi Technologies\nEthernet: Ethernet is a networking technology standardized by the IEEE (Institute of Electrical and\nElectronics Engineers) that includes the protocol, port, cable, and chip for connecting devices to a\nLAN. It enables high-speed data transmission via coaxial or fiber optic cables, with types like Fast\nEthernet (100 Mbps), Gigabit Ethernet (1 or 10 Gbps). This technology organizes data transmission\nusing frames, including source and destination addresses and error-checking codes.\nWi-Fi: Wi-Fi is a wireless networking technology based on IEEE 802.11 standards, allowing devices to\nconnect to a LAN without physical cables. It enables high-speed internet and network connections\nusing radio waves. Wi-Fi networks provide flexibility and mobility, making them ideal for various\nspaces. This technology supports protocols and speeds like 802.11ac (up to 1 Gbps) and the latest Wi-\nFi 7 (802.11be).\nNote: both wired and wireless networking technologies use frames at the Data Link Layer 2 to organize\nand manage data transmission.",
      "Frames, Packets and Segments/Datagrams\nFrames are units of data in the Data Link Layer 2 (e.g., WiFi, Bluetooth, Ethernet, etc.) and include the\nMAC addresses of the source and destination devices.\nPackets are units of data in the Network Layer 3 (e.g., IP in case of the Internet) and include the IP\naddresses of the source and destination devices.\nSegments/Datagrams are units of data in the Transport Layer 4 (TCP/UDP). Segments specifically refer\nto the data units used in TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) for reliable communication, while\nDatagrams are used in UDP (User Datagram Protocol) for connectionless communication.\nQ/A\nHub\nHub is a simple networking device that connects multiple computers in a network and broadcasts\ndata to all connected devices. Hub operates at the Physical Layer (Layer 1) of the OSI model.\nTypes of Hubs:\nPassive Hub: Does not require power and does not regenerate the signal before forwarding it.\nActive Hub: Regenerates the received signal before forwarding it, requiring a power supply.\nOperation: When a device sends data, the hub broadcasts it to all connected devices, regardless of\nthe intended recipient. This can lead to network congestion and potential performance issues,\nespecially in larger networks.",
      "Switch\nSwitch connects devices in a network and uses MAC addresses to forward data to the correct device. Switch\noperates in Data Link Layer (Layer 2) of the OSI model. Some advanced switches operate at the Network\nLayer (Layer 3). Types of Switch:\nUnmanaged Switch: Does not require power and does not regenerate the signal before forwarding it.1.\nManaged Switch: Regenerates the received signal before forwarding it, requiring a power supply.2.\nMultilayer Switches: Operates at both Layer 2 (Data Link) and Layer 3 (Network), combining the\nfunctions of a switch and a router for more advanced network management.\n3.\nOperation: Switches store MAC address information in a table known as the MAC address table or CAM\n(Content Addressable Memory) table. This table maps MAC addresses to the ports on the switch.\nIn this example: The switch receives a frame\non port 1. The destination MAC address for\nthe frame is 0000.0000.5555. The switch\nlooks up its forwarding table and finds that\nMAC address 0000.0000.5555 is recorded\non port 5. The switch then forwards the\nframe through port 5.\nWireless Access Points (WAPs)\nWireless Access Points allows wireless devices to connect to a wired network using Wi-Fi. WAPs can be\nconnected to routers or switches to provide wireless connectivity. It operates at the Data Link Layer\n(Layer 2) of the OSI model. Types of WAPs:\nStandalone WAP: Operates independently without a central controller; ideal for small networks.1.\nController-Based WAP: Managed via a central controller; for larger networks.2.\nMesh WAP: Multiple WAPs work together for seamless coverage over large areas.3.\nCloud-Managed WAP: Managed remotely via the cloud for flexibility.4.\nOperation: The WAP receives data from wireless devices and forwards it to the wired network, \n     and vice versa. It acts as a bridge between wired and wireless segments of a network.\nBroadcast Domain and Broadcast Address\nBroadcast Domain: A broadcast domain is a part of a network where any broadcast sent by a\ndevice is received by all other devices within the same segment. It defines the boundaries within\nwhich network devices can communicate directly using broadcast messages.\nBroadcast Address: A broadcast address is a special address used to send data to all devices in a\nnetwork or a specific segment. It ensures that all devices in the same broadcast domain receive\nthe message. Broadcasting addresses are located at the highest possible address within a\nnetwork. For example, in an IPv4 network, the broadcast address typically ends with \".255\" (e.g.,\n192.168.1.255).\nHop\nIn networking, a Hop refers to the trip a data packet takes from one router or intermediary\ndevice to another along its path to the final destination. This movement of data between two\nnetwork devices, such as routers, bridges, or gateways, is considered one hop. The total number\nof hops a packet travels through is called the Hop Count.\nRouter\nRouter connects multiple networks and direct data packets between different networks based on IP\naddresses and determine the best path. Routers operate in Network Layer (Layer 3) of the OSI model. \nOperation: Routers receive incoming data packets, examine the destination IP address, and forward\nthe packet to the next network along the path to its final destination.\nUnlike switches, which use MAC tables or Content Addressable Memory (CAM) tables to forward\nframes based on MAC addresses (Layer 2), routers use Routing Tables to forward packets based on IP\naddresses (Layer 3).\nShort Break",
      "Types of Routers\nWired Routers: These are traditional routers that connect devices using physical cables, typically\nEthernet cables. They are commonly used in homes and small offices to create a wired local area\nnetwork (LAN).\n1.\nWireless Routers: Use antennas to share data and create wireless local area networks (WLANs),\nenabling devices to connect to the internet without cables, typically supporting both wired and\nwireless connections.\n2.\nCore Routers: High-end routers used by service providers to direct data traffic between different\nnetworks within the internet backbone, handling large amounts of data at high speeds.\n3.\nEdge Routers: Placed at the edge or boundary of networks to connect internal networks to\nInternet service providers (ISPs) or other organizations\u2019 networks, managing incoming and\noutgoing traffic.\n4.\nVirtual Routers: Software-based routers that run on virtual machines or cloud instances, used to\ncreate virtual networks and provide flexibility in network management and scalability.\n5.\nSample Routing Table\nHere\u2019s a sample routing table, often found in routers:\nDestination: The destination network or host IP address. \nSubnet Mask: Defines the network portion of the IP address\nGateway: The IP address of the next-hop router. \nInterface: The network interface used to forward packets to the destination. \nMetric: A value used to determine the best path for data packets, with the lowest metric chosen among multiple\nroutes to the same destination. \nHop Count: The number of hops or intermediate devices the packet must pass through to reach the destination. Hop\nCount of 3 means the packet will traverse 3 network devices (like routers or switches) before reaching its\ndestination.\nGateway\nGateway is a network node that serves as an access point to another network, often used to connect\nthe local network (LAN) to external networks (e.g., the internet). \nIn most home and office networks, the gateway is the router's IP address. This gateway manages\ntraffic between your local devices and other networks, ensuring data packets are sent and received\ncorrectly.\nSo, the gateway not only routes traffic within your local network but also facilitates communication\nwith external networks.\nIP Classification\nIP classification is the method of categorizing IP addresses into different classes to efficiently\nallocate and manage IP address space. IPv4 uses a 32-bit address space, allowing for approximately\n4.3 billion unique IP addresses (2^32). There are five main classes:\nClass A: For very large networks.\nClass B: For medium-sized networks.\nClass C: For small networks.\nClass D: For multicast groups.\nClass E: Reserved for experimental purposes.\nIP Address Breakdown:\nNetwork Portion: This part of the IP address identifies the specific network to which the device\nbelongs. \nHost Portion: This part of the IP address identifies the specific device (host) within the network.",
      "IP Classes (A, B, C, D, E)\nIP classification divides IP addresses into different classes to manage and allocate them efficiently.\nThe value of the first octet determines the class. Here's a brief explanation of each class:\nClass A: 1.0.0.0 to 126.255.255.255\n127.0.0.0 to 127.255.255.255 range is reserved for loopback addresses, \n        which are used for diagnostics and testing on the local machine\nClass B: 128.0.0.0 to 191.255.255.255\nClass C: 192.0.0.0 to 223.255.255.255\nClass D: 224.0.0.0 to 239.255.255.255\nClass E: 240.0.0.0 to 255.255.255.255\nVLANs (Virtual Local Area Networks)\n VLAN is a logical grouping of devices on a network, allowing them to communicate as if on the same\nphysical network, regardless of location. VLANs segment and manage traffic efficiently, improving\nperformance, enhancing security by isolating sensitive data, and simplifying network management. They\noperate at Data Link Layer (Layer 2) of the OSI model.  VLANs separate networks \"virtually\" rather than\nphysically.\nVLAN tagging adds a tag to network frames to identify and separate traffic for different VLANs.\nTrunking transmits multiple VLANs over a single link using VLAN tags, optimizing resources and\nsimplifying management.\nSubnetting\nSubnetting creates logical segments of a network based on IP address ranges, reducing broadcast\ntraffic. It involves dividing a larger network into smaller sub-networks (subnets), which are separated\nby routers to manage and reduce network traffic. Subnetting operates at the Network Layer (Layer 3)\nof the OSI model.\nWhile subnets logically separate networks, physical separation is achieved\nthrough devices like routers. Layer 3 switches can also be used for physical\nseparation, as they combine the functionality of switches and routers, allowing\nthem to route traffic between different subnets or VLANs.\nVirtualization Essentials\nVirtualization is a technology that creates virtual versions of physical resources like servers, storage,\nand networks, enabling multiple VMs to run independently on a single machine. It improves hardware\nutilization, cuts costs, and simplifies management.\nHypervisor s software, firmware, or hardware that creates and manages VMs by abstracting\nhardware, allowing multiple operating systems to run simultaneously on a single machine, with each\nVM isolated.\nTypes of Hypervisors:\nType 1 (Bare-Metal): Runs directly on physical hardware without a host OS.\n                                                  Examples: VMware ESXi, Microsoft Hyper-V, Xen\nType 2 (Hosted): Runs on a host OS that provides virtualization services.\n                                          Examples: VMware Workstation, Oracle VirtualBox, Microsoft Hyper-V",
      "Virtualization in Infrastructure\nAdvantages of Virtualization in Infrastructure:\nEfficient Resource Usage: Optimizes hardware by hosting multiple VMs on a single server.\nCost Savings: Reduces the need for physical hardware, lowering expenses.\nScalable Solutions: Easily scale up or down to meet demand without physical hardware changes.\nEnhanced Disaster Recovery: Simplified VM backup and restoration for improved business continuity.\nIncreased Agility: Quick deployment and reconfiguration of VMs to adapt to changing needs.\nImproved Security: Isolation of VMs enhances security and minimizes breach impact.\nCentralized Management: Simplifies management and maintenance of infrastructure.\nVirtualization in Cybersecurity\nVirtualization plays a crucial role in enhancing cybersecurity by providing several benefits:\nIsolation: Contains systems and applications separately to limit the impact of breaches.\nRapid Deployment: Enables quick provisioning of VMs, enhancing response times to threats.\nTesting & Experimentation: Provides a safe space for testing security measures and tools.\nDisaster Recovery: Allows easy replication and restoration of VMs for business continuity.\nSecurity Patching: Simplifies applying updates across multiple systems, improving security.\nSandboxing: Isolates and analyzes suspicious files or code, preventing malware spread.\nQuestions and Conclusion"
