      "Networking and Operating system fundamentals\n420-ZX6-UM\nInstructor: Ehsan Shirazi\nMontreal College of Information Technology \n Coll\u00e8ge des technologies de l\u2019information de Montr\u00e9al\nEmail: ehsan.shirazi@faculty.montrealcollege.ca\nSession Learning Outcomes\nIntroduction to NAS and SAN\nDefinition of Web Servers\nUnderstanding Different Types of Web Contents\nExploring HTTP, HTTPS, SSL and TLS Protocols\nIntroduction to Containers in Computing\nExploring RAID Technology\nNetwork Attached Storage (NAS)\nNetwork Attached Storage (NAS): is a piece of storage attached to the network via a network cable, or\na wireless device acting as a NAS. A NAS contains multiple hard disks, which can be set up for\nredundancy, or various different configurations \u2014 based on your business needs.\nNAS Benefits:\nCost-effective\n24/7 accessible and remote data availability\nEasily expandable\nRedundant storage architecture\nAutomatic backups to other devices and the cloud\nFlexibility\nStorage Area Network (SAN)\nStorage Area Network (SAN): is a high-speed network or subnetwork of storage devices that can be\naccessed by multiple servers or computers, providing a shared pool of storage space.\nSAN Benefits:\nExtremely fast data access\nSeparate network for storage, reducing LAN traffic\nEasily and Highly expandable\nOS level (block level) access to files.\nExcellent performance for demanding applications like video editing\nBlock-Level Access: The SAN provides block-level access, it means SAN\nlets applications access small chunks of data (blocks) directly from the\nstorage, just like it\u2019s part of the server\u2019s own hard drive\nQ/A\nRAID (Redundant Array of Independent Disks)\nRAID (Redundant Array of Independent Disks) is a data storage technology that combines multiple\nphysical disk drives into a single logical unit to enhance performance, reliability, and fault tolerance.\nBy dividing data into chunks and storing it across drives in various patterns (known as RAID levels),\nRAID improves data redundancy, availability, and access speeds. Depending on the RAID level, data\ncan be mirrored for redundancy, striped for performance, or both.\nEach RAID level is tailored to meet specific\nneeds related to performance, fault tolerance,\nand storage efficiency, making it a valuable\ncomponent of modern data storage solutions.\nRAID 0 (Striping)\nData is split into equal-sized blocks and written across all drives in the array (striped). There is no\nredundancy, meaning if one drive fails, all data is lost.\nBenefits:\nMaximizes performance (increased speed) by writing and reading from multiple disks\nsimultaneously.\nFull use of the disk capacity (no redundancy overhead).\nDrawbacks: No fault tolerance; if one drive fails, all data is lost.\nRAID 1 (Mirroring)\nData is written identically to two or more drives (mirrored). This provides redundancy, as the same\ndata is stored on both drives.\nBenefits:\nHigh fault tolerance; if one drive fails, data is still available from the mirrored drive.\nRead speed can improve, as data can be read from either drive.\nDrawbacks: Storage capacity is halved, as data is duplicated across drives.\nRAID 5 (Striping with Parity)\nData is striped across all disks, with one drive storing parity (error-checking) data. If one drive fails,\nthe data can be rebuilt using the parity information from the remaining drives.\nBenefits:\nGood balance between performance, redundancy, and storage efficiency.\nCan survive one disk failure without data loss.\nDrawbacks: Write performance can suffer due to the need to calculate and write parity data.\nRAID 10 (Mirroring and Striping)\nCombines RAID 1 and RAID 0. It mirrors data across pairs of drives, and the mirrors are striped for\nbetter performance. It requires at least four drives.\nBenefits:\nHigh performance due to striping.\nHigh fault tolerance due to mirroring (can tolerate multiple disk failures as long as each failure is\nin different mirrored pairs).\nDrawbacks: Requires twice the number of drives compared to RAID 5, reducing the available\nstorage capacity.\nQ/A\nBreak Time",
